# .leakignore — allowed private-path references in this public repo.
#
# Format: <path-glob> <tag[,tag...]>   # reason
#
# The leak scanner (scripts/check-no-private-leaks.sh) ignores matches where
# BOTH the file path matches the glob AND the leak tag is listed. Unknown
# leaks still fail CI.
#
# Why this file exists: some skills in this repo are Studio-internal helpers
# that intentionally target the RevFleet filesystem layout. They are
# not generically installable — the README makes this explicit. Every
# entry below must have a real reason; "convenience" is not one.

# The 2026-07-03 audit sanitized the coordination-path leaks: checkpoint /
# doctor now reference paths via the $JV_REPO variable (session-state.sh),
# the deprecated revealui-handoff skill was unpublished, and README / the
# checkpoint ADR / sync-rules prose were cleaned. Entries below are the
# residual, deliberate exceptions — do NOT broaden them to re-cover a
# sanitized path; fix the source instead.

# Continuity skills — Studio-internal by design (see README).
skills/revealui-recover/**       private-jv-repo,private-jv-name,lts-drive   # targets fleet layout
skills/revealui-sync-lts/**      lts-drive,private-jv-name                    # backup target is LTS drive
skills/revealui-skills-test/**   private-jv-repo                              # validates both public + internal skills
skills/revealui-checkpoint/**    private-jv-name                              # §3b PR-scan loop iterates the private fleet repo by name (functional; paths sanitized to $JV_REPO)

# By-design: the issue/PR-body leak-scan gitleaks config (consumed by
# .github/workflows/issue-leak-scan.yml) must name the private-path and
# username literals in its detection regexes + keywords to catch them in
# issue bodies. This is the config that enforces the rule, not a leak of
# it (mirrors the ci.yml anti-regression entries).
.gitleaks.issues.toml                   lit-username,abs-home-path,private-jv-repo,private-jv-name
